    "statement": "51.7% of both-named regions are named differently by the two tools; 47.0% once three Bakta fallback-naming conventions that disagree by construction are excluded. Both numbers should be published together."
  },
  "mechanism": {
    "bakta_evidence_depth": {
      "excludes": "the fallback-naming families, which are ~100% disagreement by construction and would drive every bucket",
      "buckets": {
        "2_the_minimum": {
          "n": 41308,
          "n_disagree": 18255,
          "rate": 0.4419
        },
        "3": {
          "n": 2724,
          "n_disagree": 2174,
          "rate": 0.7981
        },
        "4_or_more": {
          "n": 1684,
          "n_disagree": 1057,
          "rate": 0.6277
        }
      },
      "finding": "Disagreement RISES with Bakta cross-reference count, from 44.2% at the minimum two to 79.8% at three. This is the opposite of the thin-evidence chain. A third cross-reference is typically an EC number, a BlastRules hit, a virulence-factor or insertion-sequence match, and it comes with a MORE specific Bakta name -- which is then more likely to differ from Prokka's more generic one."
    },
    "prokka_evidence_depth": {
      "by_ec": {
        "with_ec": {
          "n": 26861,
          "n_disagree": 11306,
          "rate": 0.4209
        },
        "without_ec": {
          "n": 18855,
          "n_disagree": 10180,
          "rate": 0.5399
        }
      },
      "by_similarity_hit": {
        "with_similarity_hit": {
          "n": 42307,
          "n_disagree": 19001,
          "rate": 0.4491
        },
        "without_similarity_hit": {
          "n": 3409,
          "n_disagree": 2485,
          "rate": 0.729
        }
      },
      "finding": "On Prokka's side the thin-evidence direction does hold: 54.0% disagreement without an EC number against 42.1% with one."
    },
    "conclusion": "The chain holds for the fallback-naming families and on Prokka's side, and reverses on Bakta's. There are two mechanisms pulling in opposite directions, not one. bakta_n_dbxref is the forest's strongest feature because extra Bakta evidence predicts a more specific Bakta name that Prokka does not match -- not because thin evidence predicts a fallback name. The db-derived features and the naming families are related, but they are not two views of a single mechanism."
  },
